# Today I Learned — Intl.ListFormat (2026-03-01)

Intl.ListFormat provides locale-aware, human-friendly joins (e.g. "a, b, and c").
Useful for UI labels, accessibility, and proper punctuation across locales.

Example:
```js
const items = ['apples', 'oranges', 'bananas'];
// English, long (adds Oxford comma)
new Intl.ListFormat('en', { style: 'long', type: 'conjunction' }).format(items);
// -> "apples, oranges, and bananas"
// Short style (compact)
new Intl.ListFormat('en', { style: 'short', type: 'conjunction' }).format(items);
// -> "apples, oranges & bananas"
// Disjunction (uses "or")
new Intl.ListFormat('en', { type: 'disjunction' }).format(['a', 'b']);
// -> "a or b"
// Fallback to simple join if not supported
const formatList = (list, opts = {}) =>
  (typeof Intl !== 'undefined' && Intl.ListFormat
    ? new Intl.ListFormat(typeof navigator !== 'undefined' ? navigator.language : 'en', opts).format(list)
    : list.join(', '));
console.log(formatList(items, { style: 'long', type: 'conjunction' }));
```

Tip: Pass the user's locale (e.g., navigator.language) to respect local grammar and conjunction words.